course: politics
lesson: 23
title: Why wars happen and why they are hard to stop; the UN and NATO
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A war is armed fighting between countries or large armed groups, often lasting years. Unlike a playground scrap, many ordinary people, called civilians, lose their homes, their safety or their lives.
explain: Countries quarrel over land, power, money, fear and old grudges. Most quarrels are settled by talking, deals and courts; war comes when talking fails or one side decides to use force.
explain: A ceasefire is like both teams agreeing to stop the match for a while: it is a pause, not the final score. Peace talks about land, safety and justice can take years, and a smile for the cameras does not mean a deal.
explain: After the Second World War, countries built the United Nations so they could talk instead of fight. NATO is a different thing: a group of North American and European countries that promise to defend each other.
explain: Words from the scenes: «back in my day» = when I was young; «kiddo» = kid; «sound» (Liverpool) = good, fine, «end of» = no more arguing; «mid» = so-so, «it's giving» = it looks like; «aye» (Scotland) = yes, «wee» = small; «I'm literally dead» = that's too funny or shocking.
fact: A war is armed fighting between countries or large armed groups; civilians are ordinary people who are not soldiers.
fact: Most quarrels between countries are settled by diplomacy, which means talking and making deals.
fact: A ceasefire is an agreement to stop fighting for a while; it is not the same as a peace deal, and ceasefires sometimes break down.
fact: Peace talks can take months or years, and each side often says the other broke a ceasefire first.
fact: The United Nations was set up in 1945 after the Second World War; 193 countries are members, and in the General Assembly each has one vote.
fact: General Assembly votes show what most of the world thinks but usually cannot force a country to obey.
fact: The Security Council has 15 members; five are permanent — the United States, the United Kingdom, France, Russia and China — and any of the five can block a decision with a veto.
fact: Some say countries such as India or African nations should get permanent seats; others say more vetoes would block even more; changing the rules needs the permanent members to agree.
fact: UN peacekeepers wear blue helmets and help keep a ceasefire without taking sides; Ireland has sent peacekeepers for decades, including to Lebanon.
fact: NATO members promise that an attack on one counts as an attack on all; Finland joined in 2023 and Sweden in 2024, news reports said.
fact: Russia's government says NATO growing towards its borders threatens Russia; NATO members say countries choose freely to join and NATO is only for defence.
fact: The news shows the most frightening things from anywhere in the world, so a war can feel close even when it is very far away.
fact: Real wars last years and hurt families; soldiers who come home often say war is nothing like games or films.
exercise: What is a war? | Armed fighting between countries or large groups | A very loud argument between two children in a playground | Any football match between two countries | A game played with toy soldiers on the floor
exercise: Who usually suffers most in a war? | Ordinary people, called civilians | Only the generals who plan the battles | Only the leaders who started the war | Nobody, as long as the war is short
exercise: How are most quarrels between countries settled? | By talking, deals and courts | By a war every single time, because nothing else works | By tossing a coin in front of the cameras | By whichever leader shouts the loudest
exercise: Noah hears «ceasefire» and cheers «The war's over!» What does a ceasefire really mean? | Both sides agree to stop fighting for a while | The war is finished forever and every question is settled | One side has won everything and the other has given up | All the soldiers go home that very day
exercise: Priya sees leaders smile, then reads «Talks end with no deal». Why? | Smiling is politeness; the hard questions were still not agreed | They agreed everything but simply forgot to sign the papers at the end | Smiling for the cameras means the war has ended | Leaders are not allowed to frown on TV, by law
exercise: When was the United Nations set up? | In 1945, after the Second World War | In 1066, after a famous battle in England | In 2001, just after the internet began | In 1900, before any of the world wars began
exercise: How many votes does each country get in the UN General Assembly? | One, whether it is big or small | One for every million people living there | Only big countries get a vote; small ones just listen | One for every soldier in its army
exercise: Which five countries are permanent members of the UN Security Council? | The US, the UK, France, Russia and China | The US, Canada, Mexico, Brazil and Japan | Germany, Italy, Spain, India and Egypt | Ireland, Australia, Kenya, Chile and Fiji
exercise: What is a veto at the Security Council? | The power of one permanent member to block a decision | A vote that every country must win before it can speak at the UN | A prize given each year for the best peace plan | A blue helmet worn by UN soldiers
exercise: What is one argument for keeping the veto? | It keeps the most powerful countries inside the UN | It lets every small country block everything it does not like | It makes the UN decide much faster than before | It was only invented last year, so it should be tried
exercise: What is one argument for changing the Security Council? | The world has changed since 1945, and countries like India want a say | It already has far too many permanent members from Africa and South America | It has never made a single decision since it began | Its building in New York is too small for meetings
exercise: What do UN peacekeepers like Finn's uncle do? | Help keep a ceasefire and protect people without taking sides | Fight on one side to win wars for the UN and its friends | Collect taxes from every country for the UN | Guard only the UN building in New York and nothing else
exercise: What is NATO's key promise? | An attack on one member counts as an attack on all | Every member must attack its neighbours when NATO says so | Every member must leave the UN once it joins NATO | Members share one army, one flag and one president
exercise: When did Finland and Sweden join NATO? | Finland in 2023 and Sweden in 2024 | Both of them joined together in 1945 | Both in 1066, long before NATO began | Neither has joined; both stayed out on purpose
exercise: Grace saw scary war pictures and cannot sleep. What helps? | Talking to a trusted grown-up and taking a break from screens | Watching more war news late at night until she gets used to it | Keeping the worry a secret so nobody else gets upset | Deciding the war will reach her house tomorrow
exercise: Tyler shouts «We won the war!» in a game. What did his teacher want them to think about? | Real wars hurt families for years and are not a game | War games are exactly like real war, only smaller and safer | Real soldiers always win quickly, just like in games | Nobody is ever hurt in real wars
test: Grandad says: «back in my day we didn't need a UN, kiddo, countries just sorted it out.» What is true? | The UN was set up in 1945 after a terrible world war so countries could talk | The UN has existed since Roman times, long before Grandad was born | Countries never fought each other before the UN | The UN is an army that wins wars for its members
test: A boy in Liverpool says: «NATO's sound, if anyone hits Finland, everyone hits back, end of.» Is he right? | Roughly: members promise to treat an attack on one as an attack on all | No, NATO is a football league and Finland is not in it | No, NATO only protects Russia and nobody else | Yes, and NATO can attack any country it likes, whenever it likes, without a reason
test: A girl says: «the veto is so mid, one country says no and it's giving "Mum says no".» What is the fair summary? | Some say the veto is unfair; others say it keeps big powers in the UN | Everyone agrees the veto is fair, so there is nothing to argue about | Every country at the UN has a veto, big or small | The veto was abolished last year by a vote
test: What would happen if every Security Council member voted yes except one permanent member, which used its veto? | The decision would be blocked | The decision would pass anyway, because most voted yes | That country would be thrown out of the UN at once | The vote would be counted again until it passed
test: A wee cousin in Glasgow says: «aye, the war's finished, the radio said ceasefire.» How do you correct it? | A ceasefire is a pause; a war ends with a lasting deal | Right, a ceasefire always ends a war forever, with nothing more to agree | A ceasefire means more fighting than there was before | Ceasefire is the name of a brand-new weapon
test: In a cartoon, two kings end a war with one arm-wrestle in an afternoon. What is the real version? | Ending a war takes long talks about land, safety and justice | Wars are always settled by sport, like arm-wrestling or football | Real kings and presidents decide wars in a single day | Wars end when one leader gets tired of them
test: Someone snaps: «shut up about talks, they just smile for the cameras and do nothing.» What is fair? | Talks are slow and often fail, but they are how wars are usually ended | Talks always fix a war in one day | Talks have never ended any war in history, so they are pointless | Leaders only ever smile when they have won
test: Why is the old helmet Finn's uncle shows him blue? | Blue helmets show they are UN peacekeepers, not fighting for a side | Blue is the colour of the Irish army only, so all its helmets are blue | Blue helmets are made of a special metal that stops all bullets | Blue helmets mean the soldier is a general
test: Which sentence reports the argument about NATO fairly? | Russia's government calls NATO's growth a threat; NATO says it is a defence alliance countries choose to join | NATO and Russia both agree that NATO is a threat, so there is no argument between them at all | Nobody anywhere has any view about NATO | NATO is a football club that Russia dislikes
test: A young teacher laughs: «I'm literally dead, the kids think war's like their video game.» What would a veteran most likely say? | Real war lasts years and hurts families; it's nothing like a game | Real war is exactly like a video game, only louder | Soldiers who fall come back to life, just like in games | Real wars are over in one round of fighting, then everyone goes home
test: Grace asks: «Will the war come here?» What is a kind, true answer? | It is far away, grown-ups are working to keep you safe, and feeling worried is normal | Yes, it will arrive tonight, so pack a bag | Nobody is allowed to talk about it, so do not ask | Wars only happen in films, never in real life
test: Chloe asks: «Can't they just talk?» What is true? | Most quarrels are settled by talking; war comes when talking fails or one side uses force | Countries never talk to each other at all | Talking always causes wars, so leaders should stay quiet | Only children can stop wars by talking to their friends in other countries
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a ceasefire? | An agreement whereby the parties suspend hostilities for a period, which does not of itself constitute peace | A final settlement determining all questions of territory, security and justice between the parties | A declaration that one party has achieved total victory | A treaty establishing a new international organisation
exam: State the year in which the United Nations was established. | Nineteen forty-five | Nineteen hundred and fourteen | Nineteen eighty-nine | Two thousand and one
exam: Identify the power vested in each permanent member of the Security Council. | The power to block a decision by means of a veto | The power to command the armies of all member states in time of war | The power to expel any member from the General Assembly | The power to cast one hundred votes in the General Assembly
exam: Select the response consistent with the established principle of the North Atlantic Treaty Organization. | An armed attack upon one member shall be regarded as an attack upon all | Each member is obliged to attack its neighbours upon instruction | Members are required to withdraw from the United Nations | Membership is restricted to countries situated in Asia and Africa
